    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nThere is a dedicated status line item `%*`/`%##` for resetting highlighting to the context-dependent default top-level highlight, e.g. `StatusLine[NC]`. But there is growing interest in more complicated stacked highlighting changes, see #35806, #26037, #29980, and there are existing hierarchies like `Normal -> TabLineFill -> TabLine -> TabLineSel -> Title`.\r\n\r\nAs an example, let's say the user wants a different background for the whole right side of the status line:\r\n```vim\r\nhi User1 guibg=green\r\nlet &stl=substitute(&stl,'%= ','%= %#User1#','')\r\n```\r\nOne problem is that the diagnostic highlights don't combine with the green background. Another problem is that whenever the diagnostics section is visible, it resets highlighting with `%##` and everything to its right loses the green background:\r\n<img width=\"500\" height=\"264\" alt=\"stl-hl-scope\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f44e59bb-740b-4189-9656-8fdea6955cc6\" />\r\nThe fix is not complicated, but it's annoying that `%#User1#` has to be repeated after the diagnostics section.\r\n\r\nAlso, that fix only works because the `%##` is at the very end. If only a part in the middle should deviate from the surrounding highlighting, the default could not be used and configuration with `'vim.diagnostic.Opts.Status'` would be necessary, since knowledge of the exact highlighting is necessary in order to reset it. Resetting within the diagnostics section is also necessary to fix the spaces between items, which don't look good when customised with something like background or underline. And if the diagnostic highlights are changed to combining, as proposed in #36685 and #35806, it would be better to reset after each item, so that they only combine with the surrounding highlighting, but not with each other. That way, it is possible to simultaneously combine with the green background and style only the errors with underline or bold, i.e. `hi DiagnosticError gui=underline`:\r\n<img width=\"624\" height=\"50\" alt=\"underline\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/27bfb7f1-797b-40b0-a67a-4f4196e0dc5d\" />\r\n\r\nIt's confusing if the highlighting after a section depends on whether the section is visible, but it's also confusing when the highlighting changes of a hidden section remain effective. Even more confusing: auto-hiding item groups `%(...%)` can refuse to hide depending on highlighting before the group, see #32925 and #29306. Since there are downsides either way, I don't think the proposed solution of just getting rid of the rule \"don't hide when highlighting changes within group\" sufficiently addresses the underlying issue. In my opinion, it should be made easier to reset the pre-group highlighting, so that it is possible to create a robust auto-hiding section.\r\n\r\nThere was also a related discussion in #29976 regarding the question whether `%#` should always be combining. One comment suggests that this would become unwieldy unless there was some kind of \"end delimiter\" for highlight group sections. This was before `%$` was introduced, and the fact that we now have both options makes it less important. But it would still be useful to avoid repetition, which gets worse the more `%$`s are stacked, and remove the need to keep track of each level's highlighting to be able to pop from the stack. In the following example, in order to finish the underlined subsection, one has to reset with `%#User1#` and reapply the `%$User2$` modification:\r\n<img width=\"89\" height=\"38\" alt=\"nesting\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ca43e531-9086-4786-9234-067ba09406b0\" />\r\n```vim\r\nhi User1 guibg=green\r\nhi User2 guifg=red\r\nhi User3 gui=underline\r\nset stl=a%#User1#b%$User2$c%$User3$d%#User1#%$User2$e%#User1#f%##g\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nIntroduce highlighting scopes `%#(...%#)` that clean up after themselves, i.e. reset highlighting at the end to whatever it was just before. This enables creating self-contained snippets that can be used e.g. as\r\n- examples in documentation/discussions to be copy-pasted\r\n- reusable modules in status line plugins or the default status line\r\n\r\nwhich integrate well without breaking their surroundings when inserted. In particular, it makes `vim.diagnostic.status()` easier to reuse in custom configurations, and it makes the default status line more robust under slight modifications. It allows to easily protect sections when content is injected from an \"untrusted\" place like `'vim.diagnostic.Opts.Status'`. And it reduces duplication and bookkeeping, the above example can now be written like this:\r\n```vim\r\nset stl=a%#(%#User1#b%#(%$User2$c%#(%$User3$d%#)e%#)f%#)g\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote on the syntax: I chose it because it's easy to remember and possibly even to guess the meaning, I think that the hl-family of stl items already uses up a lot of symbols (`#`, `$`, `*`) and I didn't want to waste the last paren-like ASCII character pair that doesn't have a meaning yet (`%[...%]`) if there's a good alternative. Luckily, highlight group names cannot contain parentheses.",
